1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:04,859
No. 7 · The Roman dodecahedron.

2
00:00:05,359 --> 00:00:07,949
The Romans wrote about almost everything.

3
00:00:07,989 --> 00:00:12,179
How to build. How to run an army. Even what to eat.

4
00:00:12,479 --> 00:00:15,286
But they never once mentioned these.

5
00:00:16,486 --> 00:00:19,916
Across the old northern provinces of the empire,

6
00:00:19,956 --> 00:00:23,776
archaeologists have found about 130 of them.

7
00:00:23,816 --> 00:00:26,746
Hollow, cast in bronze.

8
00:00:27,046 --> 00:00:29,896
Twelve flat faces, each one a pentagon.

9
00:00:29,936 --> 00:00:34,309
A knob on every corner. And a hole in every face.

10
00:00:34,309 --> 00:00:37,177
But the holes are not the same size.

11
00:00:37,777 --> 00:00:41,047
One found in Hertfordshire, England, in 2018

12
00:00:41,087 --> 00:00:44,347
is 82 mm across.

13
00:00:44,387 --> 00:00:48,442
Its holes range from under 14 mm to 20.

14
00:00:49,042 --> 00:00:51,512
The smallest are about 4 cm, the size of a golf ball.

15
00:00:51,552 --> 00:00:57,009
The largest, 11. Nearly a grapefruit.

16
00:00:57,709 --> 00:01:00,559
And yet, no Roman text describes them.

17
00:01:00,599 --> 00:01:04,546
No painting shows one. No carving depicts one.

18
00:01:04,946 --> 00:01:07,856
Historians have thrown every theory at them.

19
00:01:07,896 --> 00:01:11,536
Candle holders. Surveying tools. Dice.

20
00:01:11,576 --> 00:01:14,153
Even a gadget for knitting gloves.

21
00:01:14,253 --> 00:01:16,503
Every theory has a problem.

22
00:01:16,543 --> 00:01:18,623
A measuring tool should be standard,

23
00:01:18,663 --> 00:01:21,377
but no two of these are made the same.

24
00:01:22,177 --> 00:01:25,527
The Hunt Museum in Ireland holds another one.

25
00:01:25,567 --> 00:01:27,907
Its catalogue says it plainly:

26
00:01:27,947 --> 00:01:31,087
there is no mention of them in classical sources,

27
00:01:31,127 --> 00:01:33,984
and their purpose remains unclear.

28
00:01:34,584 --> 00:01:38,134
And several were found buried in hoards of coins.

29
00:01:38,174 --> 00:01:41,780
You don't bury a candle holder with your life savings.

30
00:01:42,780 --> 00:01:45,290
Whoever owned these thought they mattered.

31
00:01:45,330 --> 00:01:47,490
And then they vanished from history,

32
00:01:47,530 --> 00:01:50,987
leaving us nothing but the objects themselves.
